EACH WAYPOINT IS A POINT (kogaki#1326): its `point` is the proposition its paragraph makes, in the article's subject, one sentence -- it names what the paragraph establishes, never how the reader is to feel or what they are handed or receive, and it is never the Move's specialized-by-the-Leg role with the subject filled in. A WAYPOINT'S POINT IS READ ALONE (kogaki#1449): a later stage reads each waypoint's `point` by itself, never beside the other waypoints, so the point names what it refers to in full -- it carries no "this", "that", "these", "those", "it" or bare definite noun whose referent is another waypoint -- and uses no term that is not in its Claim's served material or the Persona's prior knowledge, since a Leg introduces no term (kogaki#1768); a point opening with one of those words, or carrying "this <noun>" or "these <noun>" whose noun is in no Claim of the Leg, is refused.
